Here is an example implementation of a class that dynamically manages the parsing, validation, and caching of large XML documents:
```
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.Schema;
namespace XmlManager {
    public class XmlCache {
        private readonly string _xmlUrl;
        private readonly string _xsdPath;
        private XmlDocument _document;
        private DateTime _lastUpdated;
        public XmlCache(string xmlUrl, string xsdPath) {
            _xmlUrl = xmlUrl;
            _xsdPath = xsdPath;
            _lastUpdated = DateTime.MinValue;
        }
        public async Task<XmlDocument> GetDocumentAsync() {
            var now = DateTime.Now;
            if (_document == null || (now - _lastUpdated).TotalHours > 1) {
                await UpdateCacheAsync();
            }
            return _document;
        }
        private async Task UpdateCacheAsync() {
            using (var client = new HttpClient()) {
                var xmlStream = await client.GetStreamAsync(_xmlUrl);
                using (var reader = XmlReader.Create(xmlStream)) {
                    if (_xsdPath != null) {
                        var schemaSet = new XmlSchemaSet();
                        schemaSet.Add(null, _xsdPath);
                        reader.ValidationType = ValidationType.Schema;
                        reader.SchemaValidationEventHandler += (sender, e) => {
                            throw new InvalidOperationException(e.Message);
                        };
                    }
                    var xmlDoc = new XmlDocument();
                    await xmlDoc.LoadAsync(reader);
                    _document = xmlDoc;
                    _lastUpdated = DateTime.Now;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This class takes in the URL of the XML data source and the path to an XSD schema validator, and provides a method for asynchronously retrieving the cached XML document. The cache is updated every hour by default, but can be configured to update more or less frequently.
The `UpdateCacheAsync` method uses `HttpClient` to download the XML data from the URL, and then parses it using `XmlReader`. If an XSD schema validator is provided, it adds the schema to a `XmlSchemaSet` and validates the XML against it. Any validation errors throw an exception.
The class also includes dependency injection support by allowing the caller to pass in their own `HttpClient` implementation if needed. This can be useful for testing or integrating with other libraries that provide custom HTTP implementations.
Overall, this implementation provides a flexible and scalable solution for managing large XML documents in a high-traffic e-commerce application, while ensuring data freshness and minimizing memory usage.

